<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=egyptian-blue">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Egyptian blue</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3250 BCE</span> · Also known as Calcium copper silicate, Cuprorivaite, Caeruleum, Blue frit</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Egyptian blue, a calcium <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> silicate, is a pigment made by heating silica, lime, a copper compound and an alkali, and is considered the first synthetic pigment. The earliest evidence of its use is on an alabaster bowl of the late pre-dynastic period, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqada">Naqada</a> III, about 3250 BCE, excavated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekhen">Hierakonpolis</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> made it to imitate turquoise and lapis lazuli and painted it on tombs, wall paintings, furnishings and statues; it is also found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>, the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. Vitruvius described how it was made in the first century BCE, but its use died out in the fourth century CE and the method was forgotten until modern analysis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/egyptian-blue.jpg" alt="Egyptian blue" width="330" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FK1954 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egyptian_blue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 3250 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Hierakonpolis, Egypt</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The pigment is also said to have been first synthesised in the Fourth Dynasty. Whether its presence beyond Egypt reflects its spread or parallel invention is unclear.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=egyptian-blue">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
